 * **A count of 2329 passed and 135 skipped (from the project owner's own computer):** its cause is not known, and no figure here confirms it. The nearest fit, as an inference only, is a run without Node on the path (Node missing skips 22 tests in the eight files above), but the totals do not match a full run of this commit (2516 tests; 2329 + 135 = 2464), so the two numbers are not reconciled. What is unknown: that computer's operating system, whether `gitleaks` was installed there, whether Node was present, and the exact commit. If you see a count near 2329/135, run `python -m pytest -q -rs` and look for "node is not installed".
 
+## Tests of bad settings, the start scripts and the exit codes
+
+A bad `DESK_*` value must end every program with one plain line and an exit code, never a Python traceback, and never print the value. The files: `tests/test_bad_setting_entry_points.py` (every program run with bad values; the value-echo checks at every refusal site of the desk; paths that are not valid text), `tests/test_exit_code_table.py` (the one table of exit codes, by program; programs are run and their codes looked up; every row names the test that produces it or is listed as not produced; the table printed in this folder's `settings.md` is compared with the generator `tools/render_exit_table.py`; no page may repeat a program's exit numbers), `tests/test_setting_reader_guard.py` (a walk over the source of `src`, `scripts`, `load_tools`, `demo_only` and `tools`: no program reads a setting or a path outside the one reader and the one path helper, also through a name that held the text, `x or default`, `self.env` and the like; each shape is planted in the test), `tests/test_start_all_late_refusal.py` (`start_all.sh` when the demo server or the desk does not come up, including a foreign program holding the demo server's port and an IPv6 loopback address), `tests/test_late_refusal_value_echo.py` (a PIN, not proof: it plants each late refusal of the desk with a stand-in message and shows the line a person sees is scrubbed; the real messages are checked in the files above), `tests/test_reset_scripts.py` (the reset and start scripts with a stand-in `python`), and `tests/test_ps1_pins.py`. The last one reads the text of the two PowerShell scripts only; it does not run them (every check in it is a text pin: it proves the text of the script, not what Windows does with it). Nothing here has run the PowerShell scripts: that needs Windows (the Windows-only cases in that file are skipped everywhere else).
+
